00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:07.349 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  material samples brought back from  objects in space can shed light on  billions of years of our solar system's  history including details about the  origin of life here on earth  a piece of an asteroid is a piece of the  earliest beginnings of the solar system  4.6 billion years ago when our solar  system formed initially there was just  gas and dust swirling around and that  coalesced  into planetesimals and those things  crashed into each other and got bigger  and some became planets and those that  did not  remain as asteroids and they're like  little snapshots of earth's beginnings  but picking up rocks from the surface of  an asteroid or planetary satellite isn't  as easy as it sounds  it requires voyages of millions of miles  to bodies that have never been seen up  close  probes need to land on surfaces with  weak gravitational pulls where the  surface terrain is completely unknown  gather samples and then make the long  trip back  and while behemoths like nasa and spacex  grab headlines it's a relatively young  space agency  the japan aerospace exploration agency  better known as jaxa that is taking the  giant leap necessary to bring the  mysteries of space back down to earth  [Music]  if we want to find out about the origin  of our solar system or the origin of  life itself  we need to study materials that were  around 4.6 billion years ago when the  solar system was born  meteorites actually fit that bill and  luckily the earth is lousy with them  meteorites make impacts with our planet  all the time there's just one problem  itself was officially founded in 2003  when three different national space  agencies were merged into one  prior agencies in japan had excelled in  infrared and x-ray astronomy and the  design and launch of communications  satellites and rockets  jaxa continued those efforts as it began  devoting money and research into sample  return  with the need for relatively untouched  materials from space clear planning for  the world's first sample return from an  asteroid began in 1995.  on may 9 2003 the robotic spacecraft  hayabusa meaning falcon in english  launched  with its path set for the asteroid  itakawa a near-earth asteroid located  between mars and earth  using its four ion engines for the one  million two hundred thousand mile plus  journey hayabusa made contact with  itakawa more than two years after its  launch in september of 2005.  that is a special challenge these  asteroids are not as large as planets  and they don't have  the gravity to allow you to just land so  you have to figure out how to land and  stick there and then have your your  instrument your drill or your collector  operate in those conditions  not everything went according to plan  weeks were spent trying and failing to  launch hayabusa's designated probe and  there was a fuel leak from its chemical  engine part of the difficulty with the  hayabusa mission was with the size of  itakawa itself only 1100 feet in  diameter its gravitational pull is weak  so hayabusa had to maintain heliocentric  orbit meaning the craft would track the  asteroid's path around the sun  in addition the distance from earth  required hayabusa to be fully autonomous  thanks to the communication delay  [Music]  the probe was finally able to touch down  and attempt a sample retrieval on  itakawa for 30 minutes  [Music]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  as hayabusa returned no one knew whether  the sample collection had been a success  or not but upon its arrival to earth in  june of 2010 less than one milligram of  dust from itakawa was discovered in its  collection module a truly tiny specimen  but nevertheless the first successful  asteroid sample return in history  one of the conclusions was that the  meteorite achikawa that was studied was  consistent with stony meteorites that we  have found on the earth but we couldn't  really conclude that until we could get  an actual sample from  the asteroid bring it back and make a  direct comparison  second  jaxa would expand on this flawed but  successful mission with the launch of  hayabusa 2 to the asteroid yugu in 2014.  the second asteroid sample retrieval  mission was notable not only for its  more advanced machinery and distance of  9 million miles but for its destination  unlike itakawa's inert collection of  boulder-sized rocks yugu is carbonaceous  meaning it contains some of the solar  system's most pristine untouched  materials minerals ice and organic  materials  the carbonaceous  ones are of great  interest to us especially in this recent  hayabusa2 mission because they contain  remnant organic matter and also water  that may have been  the source of organic matter to evolve  life in the source of water for the  oceans on earth  after its approach to yugu hayabusa 2  deployed a series of three small rovers  that due to the asteroid's low gravity  made a series of hops rather than roll  on wheels to survey its surface  in order to retrieve a subsurface sample  free from the weathering of space the  craft needed to fire a projectile into  the surface of the asteroid and in  another first created a man-made crater  in yugo  hayabusa 2 was then able to descend and  conduct subsurface sampling from the  site of the blast  unlike the first mission the second was  able to collect more than five grams of  material from the surface of yuku now  the largest asteroid sample ever  recovered  originally we expected to have the  samples greater than 0.1 gram but ended  up with the 4.5 grams which is more than  50 times of the glams  the next surprise was that there is the  infrared microscope found water bearing  minerals  the i abused it ii mission sampling  ryugu  has yet to yield information  because the samples came back in 2020  they've been distributed to labs all  over the world and we'll soon get  information from them on the origin of  water potentially from meteorites as  well as the nature of carbonaceous  material  jax's next sample return mission is set  to be its most ambitious and  illuminating yet and it's to an area of  the solar system that's particularly  popular at the moment the mmx or martian  moons exploration mission is slated to  visit two of mars's moons phobos and  deimos with a launch planned for 2024  mmx will visit and survey both moons  with the hope of collecting a sample  from phobos in mid-2025 and a return to  earth in 2029  mmx seeks to find whether phobos and  deimos are asteroids that became caught  in mars orbit long ago or if there were  fragments that coalesced after a major  impact with the red planet as scientists  more often suspect  there are also implications for the  origins of life in our solar system  [Music]  mmx will be humanity's first opportunity  at bringing back samples from other  planetary systems and jaxa hopes that  the round-trip visit will reveal  important information that can be used  on future manned missions to the martian  sphere including whether phobos can be  used as an orbital base  [Music]  on mmx part of the sampling will be done  with the help of us-based honeybee  robotics  meet planetvac  the planetary vacuum sucks up regolith  another name for the dirt lying on top  of the bedrock of space bodies and  collects it to either be analyzed by  onboard tools or stored away for return  to earth  the planetvac takes the place of a leg  on a lander and uses compressed gas  that's usually already on board the  craft to pressurize fuel tanks  mmx is still currently in the  development stage and jaxa says the cost  will be around 419 million dollars  if all goes according to plan jaxa can  further cement its track record as the  world's premier experts at sample return  jax's sample return missions will over  the coming decades help prepare other  space agencies for manned missions to  various types of environments in space  and as research continues on the samples  already brought back help to solve some  of the mysteries that still exist about  the formation of life on earth  the world over smaller space agencies  have found crucial niches that don't  require the massive funding of nasa blue  origin or spacex  in this way jaxa is more than holding  their own with our worlds space  heavyweights  you

